  "image_name": "f409a58664472d83ce3f18af1d52b88c.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the mercury (Hg) budget of the Arctic system, showing the main reservoirs and exchanges among land, atmosphere, ice, ocean, and sediments. The largest visible mercury stores are on land, especially permafrost, the active layer, and surface soils, with much smaller amounts in snowpacks, sea ice, and the atmosphere. Glaciers also appear to hold a substantial mercury reservoir.\n\nArrows indicate annual fluxes linking these compartments. The atmosphere exchanges mercury with both land and ocean, with deposition to the Arctic Ocean and some return emission back to the air. Rivers or coastal inputs seem to transfer mercury from soils and vegetation into the ocean. Within the ocean, mercury is distributed by depth: relatively small amounts occur in the upper 0–20 m and 20–200 m layers, while the largest oceanic reservoir is shown below 500 m. Vertical arrows suggest movement between surface and deeper waters, including downward transport from the upper ocean and some upward return.\n\nThe seafloor is another important sink. Mercury accumulates in shelf and deep sediments, with red and gray arrows indicating burial and internal sediment exchange. Overall, the diagram emphasizes that most mercury is stored in frozen soils and deep ocean/sediment compartments, while atmospheric and surface-ice reservoirs are comparatively small but active in exchange.",
